# Node.js File I/O Operations

## Overview
This skill covers reading, writing, and managing files in Node.js using both synchronous and asynchronous APIs.

## Module: fs (File System)

### Core Methods

#### Writing Files
```javascript
const fs = require('fs');

// Synchronous (blocks execution)
fs.writeFileSync(filePath, content, 'utf8');

// Asynchronous with callback
fs.writeFile(filePath, content, 'utf8', (err) => {
  if (err) throw err;
  console.log('File written');
});

// Promise-based (modern)
const fsPromises = fs.promises;
await fsPromises.writeFile(filePath, content, 'utf8');
```

#### Reading Files
```javascript
// Synchronous
const content = fs.readFileSync(filePath, 'utf8');

// Asynchronous with callback
fs.readFile(filePath, 'utf8', (err, data) => {
  if (err) throw err;
  console.log(data);
});

// Promise-based
const content = await fsPromises.readFile(filePath, 'utf8');
```

### Directory Operations
```javascript
// Check if file exists
const exists = fs.existsSync(filePath);

// Create directory
fs.mkdirSync(dirPath, { recursive: true });

// List directory
const files = fs.readdirSync(dirPath);
```

## Path Module

### Working with File Paths
```javascript
const path = require('path');

// Join paths (cross-platform)
const filePath = path.join(__dirname, 'data', 'file.txt');

// Get absolute path
const absolute = path.resolve(filePath);

// Parse path components
const parsed = path.parse(filePath);
// { root: '/', dir: '/root/data', base: 'file.txt', ext: '.txt', name: 'file' }

// Get directory name
const dir = path.dirname(filePath);

// Get file name
const name = path.basename(filePath);

// Get extension
const ext = path.extname(filePath);
```

## Large File Handling

### Streaming (for large files)
```javascript
const fs = require('fs');

// Writing stream
const stream = fs.createWriteStream(filePath);
stream.write('data chunk 1\n');
stream.write('data chunk 2\n');
stream.end();

stream.on('finish', () => {
  console.log('Write complete');
});
```

### Building Content in Memory (for moderate sizes)
```javascript
let content = '';
content += 'line 1\n';
content += 'line 2\n';
fs.writeFileSync(filePath, content, 'utf8');
```

## Error Handling

### Try-Catch with Sync
```javascript
try {
  const content = fs.readFileSync(filePath, 'utf8');
} catch (err) {
  if (err.code === 'ENOENT') {
    console.log('File not found');
  } else {
    throw err;
  }
}
```

### Promise-based Error Handling
```javascript
try {
  await fsPromises.writeFile(filePath, content);
} catch (err) {
  console.error('Write failed:', err.message);
}
```

## Common Patterns

### Ensure Directory Exists Before Writing
```javascript
const dir = path.dirname(filePath);
fs.mkdirSync(dir, { recursive: true });
fs.writeFileSync(filePath, content, 'utf8');
```

### Write and Report Success
```javascript
fs.writeFileSync(outputPath, content, 'utf8');
console.log(`Successfully wrote to ${outputPath}`);
```

## Encodings

Common text encodings:
- `'utf8'`: UTF-8 (most common)
- `'ascii'`: ASCII text
- `'binary'` or `'latin1'`: ISO-8859-1
- `'hex'`: Hexadecimal
- `'base64'`: Base64 encoding

For text files, always use `'utf8'`.

## Best Practices

1. **Always specify encoding** when working with text files
2. **Use path.join()** or path.resolve() for cross-platform compatibility
3. **Handle errors** appropriately (file not found, permission denied, etc.)
4. **For large files**, consider streams instead of loading entire file to memory
5. **Use Promise-based API** in modern async code

## Resources
- Node.js fs Module: https://nodejs.org/api/fs.html
- Node.js path Module: https://nodejs.org/api/path.html
- Node.js Stream Documentation: https://nodejs.org/api/stream.html

